File: pps-mode.info, Node: Top

PPS Mode
********

PPS Mode is a spreadsheet browser and editor for GNU Emacs.  It reads
and writes the Pinnacle Point Spreadsheet (`.pps') format and presents
the workbook as a compact keyboard driven grid.

This manual describes PPS Mode version 0.0.1.

* Menu:

* Installation::               Install and open the manual.
* Quick Start::                Open a workbook and edit cells.
* Navigation::                 Move through sheets and cells.
* Editing::                    Change cells, rows, and columns.
* Formulas::                   Enter safe Lisp shaped formulas.
* Sheets::                     Manage workbook sheets.
* Data Tools::                 Search, sort, import, and export.
* File Format::                The PPS file at a glance.
* Customization::              Display and capacity settings.
* Command Reference::          Common commands and keys.
* Copying::                    License for this manual.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: Installation

Installation
************

PPS Mode requires GNU Emacs 29.1 or newer.  Emacs 29 and later can
install the stable branch directly from GitHub.  Evaluate:

     (package-vc-install
      '(pps-mode
        :url "https://github.com/Pinnacle-Point-Development/pps-mode.git"
        :vc-backend Git
        :main-file "pps-mode.el"))

For a checkout, add its directory to `load-path' and load the package:

     (add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/pps-mode")
     (require 'pps-mode)

Files whose names end in `.pps' then open in PPS Mode automatically.
Use `M-x pps-info' to open this manual.  It is also available from the
Info directory with `C-h i m PPS Mode RET'.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: Quick Start

Quick Start
***********

Visit an existing `.pps' file with `C-x C-f'.  To make a workbook,
visit a new filename ending in `.pps'; PPS Mode creates one blank
sheet.

The current cell has a highlighted border.  Press `RET', `F2', or `e'
to edit it.  Enter plain text, a number, a boolean, a date, or a
formula as appropriate for its column type.  Save with the usual `C-x
C-s'.  Normal Emacs backups, file locking, auto-save, and revert
apply.

Press `?' for mode help and use the `Spreadsheet' menu to discover all
commands.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: Navigation

Navigation
**********

Arrow keys and `h', `j', `k', and `l' move one cell.  `TAB' and
`S-TAB' move across columns and wrap between rows.  `M-<' and `M->'
move to the first and last row.  `Home' and `End' move to the first
and last column.  Page Up and Page Down move by a rendered page.

Use `g' to enter an A1 address such as `B12', or a sheet-qualified
address such as `Rates!B2'.  `[' and `]' select the previous and next
sheet.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: Editing

Editing
*******


Cells and selections
====================

Press `SPC' on one corner, move to the opposite corner, and run a
command to act on the rectangular selection.  Press `SPC' again or
`C-g' to clear the mark.  `DEL' clears selected cells.  `F' fills
every selected cell from the top-left cell.

`M-w', `C-w', and `C-y' copy, cut, and paste rectangles.  PPS Mode
uses tab-separated text on the kill ring, so copied data can move
between the workbook and other programs.  Formula references adjust
when formula cells are copied.


Rows and columns
================

`o' inserts a row below the current row and `O' inserts one above.
`D' deletes selected rows.  `I' inserts a column before the current
one.  The `Rows and Columns' menu also inserts a column after the
current one and changes column names, types, and display widths.

Structural changes update formula references.  A reference to a
deleted cell becomes a `#REF!' error.  Use `u' and `U' for logical
undo and redo within the workbook.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: Formulas

Formulas
********

Press `=' to enter a formula.  Formula syntax resembles Lisp, with
cell references written as A1 addresses:

     =(+ B1 C1)
     =(sum (range B1 B20))
     =(if (> C1 0) "OPEN" "CLOSED")
     =(+ (cell "Rates" B2) $A$1)

Supported operations include arithmetic, comparison, logic, text
functions, numeric helpers, aggregates, cell references, and ranges.
The reader checks every formula against a fixed allowlist.  Formulas
cannot evaluate arbitrary Lisp or access files, processes, or the
network.

Dollar signs make row or column components absolute when a formula is copied.
For example, `$A1' fixes column A and `A$1' fixes row 1.  Use
`C-c =' to clear the formula cache and recalculate the workbook.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: Sheets

Sheets
******

Use `C-c s' to switch by name, `C-c a' to add a sheet, and `C-c n' to
rename one.  `C-c d' duplicates the active sheet and `C-c x' deletes
it.  A workbook always contains at least one sheet, and sheet names
are unique and case-sensitive.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: Data Tools

Data Tools
**********

`/' searches for a regular expression.  `f' filters visible rows; `C-c
C-f' clears the filter.  `s' sorts rows by the active column.  With a
prefix argument, it sorts in descending order.

`C-c +' reports the count and numeric summary of a selection.  `C-c ?'
describes the source and calculated value of the current cell.  `C-c
v' validates column types and reports formula errors.

`pps-import-delimited-file' imports CSV or TSV into a new sheet.
`pps-export-delimited-file' exports the active sheet.  Prefix
arguments enable type inference during import and calculated values
during export.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: File Format

File Format
***********

A PPS file is UTF-8 text containing one data-only S-expression.  Its
root is a `pps' form with format version 1, metadata, an active sheet
name, and a vector of sheets.  Each sheet contains column descriptions
and row vectors.  Cells can hold blanks, booleans, strings, numbers,
dates, date-times, or a validated formula tree.

The reader never passes workbook data to `eval'.  It rejects trailing
forms, unknown properties, invalid cell forms, excessive dimensions, and
unrecognized formula operators.  The complete normative contract is in
`FORMAT.md' in the distribution.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: Customization

Customization
*************

Use `M-x customize-group RET pps RET' to change PPS Mode settings.

`pps-retro-display'
     Use the black and yellow COLORMODECTL inspired display.
`pps-use-unicode-borders'
     Draw the grid with Unicode box characters.
`pps-default-column-width'
     Set the default width of columns that have no stored width.
`pps-page-size'
     Set the number of rows rendered at once.
`pps-history-limit'
     Limit the logical workbook undo history.
`pps-max-file-bytes'
     Limit accepted input size.
`pps-max-rows'
     Limit rows per sheet.
`pps-max-columns'
     Limit columns per sheet.
`pps-max-formula-range-cells'
     Limit cells expanded by one formula range.



File: pps-mode.info, Node: Command Reference

Command Reference
*****************

*Key*             *Command*                *Action*
`RET', `e'        `pps-edit-cell'          Edit the current cell.
`='               `pps-enter-formula'      Enter a formula.
`SPC'             `pps-toggle-mark'        Start or clear a selection.
`M-w'             `pps-copy'               Copy selected cells.
`C-w'             `pps-cut'                Cut selected cells.
`C-y'             `pps-paste'              Paste a rectangle.
`o', `O'          row insertion            Insert below or above.
`D'               `pps-delete-rows'        Delete selected rows.
`I'               `pps-insert-column-before' Insert a column.
`s'               `pps-sort-column'        Sort by the active column.
`/'               `pps-find'               Search cell displays.
`f'               `pps-set-filter'         Filter visible rows.
`g'               `pps-goto-cell'          Go to an A1 address.
`[}, `''          sheet navigation         Change active sheet.
`u', `U'          logical history          Undo or redo a change.
`C-c v'           `pps-validate'           Validate the workbook.
`C-c C-i'         `pps-info'               Open this manual.
`?'               `pps-help'               Describe PPS Mode.

All interactive commands are also available through `M-x' and the
`Spreadsheet' menu.



